# Migrating `TimedExecutor` to .NET 8

## Key Problem

`Thread.Abort()` and `Thread.ResetAbort()` throw `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 8. The cooperative cancellation pattern via `CancellationToken` is the replacement.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Block caller like the original Thread.Join(timeout) did
            task.Wait(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }

        // Unwrap AggregateException for consistency with original behavior
        if (task.IsFaulted)
            throw task.Exception!; // AggregateException

        return task.Result;
    }

    // Async-friendly overload (preferred)
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

## Usage Change

```csharp
// Before (.NET Framework)
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(() => LongRunning(), TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

// After (.NET 8) — work must cooperate with cancellation
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(ct =>
{
    // Check cancellation in loops / between steps
    ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
    return LongRunning(ct);
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
```

## Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **No forced termination** | Unlike `Thread.Abort()`, cancellation is *cooperative*. If `work` ignores the token, it runs to completion despite timeout. |
| **Blocking I/O** | Pass the token to `HttpClient`, `Stream.ReadAsync`, `SqlCommand`, etc. Otherwise the call won't interrupt. |
| **`default(T)` capture** | The original relied on closure mutation from a thread—`Task.Run` returns the value directly, eliminating this race. |
| **`ThreadAbortException` gone** | Replace any `catch (ThreadAbortException)` cleanup with `catch (OperationCanceledException)` or `finally` blocks. |
| **`Task.Result` deadlocks** | Never call `.Result`/`.Wait()` on a UI/sync-context thread. Use the async overload in ASP.NET/WPF/MAUI. |

## If You Truly Need Hard Termination

For legacy code that can't be refactored to accept a token (e.g., third-party libs):

```csharp
// Last resort — run in a separate process or use a timeout + abandon pattern
public T ExecuteWithHardTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    var task = Task.Run(work);

    if (task.Wait(timeout))
        return task.Result;

    // Task is abandoned (still running!) — log a warning
    throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout (abandoned)");
}
```

⚠️ This **leaks the thread** — it keeps running. True hard-kill requires a child process boundary.
